<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- eldato runner autoscale, the SAME pass the Linux cells run on a systemd timer (#2403).
         Scheduled here by scripts/install-runner-autoscale-timer.sh, which renders this file and
         bootstraps it; ONE install verb, the platform's own scheduler (#2058).

         WHY THIS FILE IS NOT IN templates/launchd/. install-launchd.sh installs EVERY
         templates/launchd/*.plist with NO arguments and a different substitution set, and
         pi-bootstrap/setup.sh runs it on every Darwin box. A copy there would render with its
         placeholders unresolved, be SKIPPED with a warning, and the box would silently have no
         scheduled pass while the installer printed nothing wrong — the inert-enforcer shape this
         whole feature exists to remove. The subdirectory keeps it out of that glob and out of
         templates/launchd/RETIRED (which errors on a label that has both). Same reason
         templates/launchd/reaper/ and templates/launchd/harness-apply/ exist.

         NO PLACEHOLDER MAY APPEAR INSIDE AN XML COMMENT. This comment is XML text: a doubled dash is
         forbidden in it, so a placeholder that renders an option flag would make the installed plist
         not parse, launchd would refuse it, and the box would silently have no pass. That is why this
         block names placeholders without braces, and why the installer refuses a rendered plist that
         does not parse.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS THE INTERPRETER ITSELF, NOT bash AND NOT A TCC LAUNCHER. The disk
         reaper's job needs a checkout's own python to launder a bash script past macOS TCC, because
         its classes run git against repos under ~/Documents. This pass touches no repo: it reads the
         runner pool over the GitHub API through gh, counts local listeners under the runner root,
         and talks to systemctl/launchctl. So there is nothing for TCC to refuse, and the direct
         interpreter is the honest artifact. gh IS reached over PATH, which is why PATH is exported
         below rather than inherited.

         NINE MINUTES, matching the systemd timer and the interval the converger renders itself on a
         cell. The pass is idempotent and cheap when there is nothing to do, and a launchd job that is
         still running does not get a second instance at the next interval.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the pass appends its own JSON record under
         RUNNER_AUTOSCALE_STATE_DIR, and an unbounded launchd-captured log is the symlink-truncation
         surface the reapers already guard. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>{{LABEL}}</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{PY_BIN}}</string>
        <string>{{SCRIPT}}</string>
        <string>--repo</string>
        <string>{{REPO}}</string>
        <!-- THE MODE ARGUMENT IS AN ELEMENT OR NOTHING, rendered by the installer. The pass has no
             "apply" flag: converging is its default and the only switch it accepts is the dry-run one, so
             an ARMED render omits this element entirely. An empty string element would be handed to the
             pass as a positional argument and every tick would exit 2 (unrecognized arguments) —
             measured on pi-cell-1 with a literal apply flag. -->
{{MODE_ARG}}
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <!-- gh is resolved through PATH by the pass, and a launchd job inherits none of a login
             shell's rc files, so the installer's PATH is exported verbatim. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- ONE record, one path: the scheduled pass and an operator's status verb read the same
             file, and the arming record lives beside it. -->
        <key>RUNNER_AUTOSCALE_STATE_DIR</key>
        <string>{{STATE_DIR}}</string>
        <!-- The provisioner pair is rendered ONLY when the install resolved one, for the reason the
             systemd unit states: the pass can REPAIR and RE-REGISTER without it but can never ADD,
             because a scheduled pass has no cwd that carries tools/install-ci-runner-x64.sh. It is
             written as ONE line at column 0: the renderer is sed, and a multi-line replacement is
             not portable to the BSD sed this installer runs under on the platform that uses THIS
             file. An empty element here would be worse than none: a variable set to the empty
             string is not an unset variable. -->
{{PROVISIONER_ENV}}
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>{{INTERVAL_SEC}}</integer>
    <!-- A pass AT LOAD as well as on the interval, so a freshly installed agent has a record within
         seconds instead of after the first interval. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- A background convergence, never a deadline: it must not compete with a lane for CPU or I/O. -->
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
